  "question": {
    "text": "Let $f:[-2,2] \\rightarrow R$ be a continuous function such that $f(x)$ assumes only irrational values. If $f(\\sqrt{2})=\\sqrt{2},$ then",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "B2Jn9",
      "text": "$f(0)=0$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "kuMht",
      "text": "$f(\\sqrt{2}-1)=\\sqrt{2}-1$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "il8zq",
      "text": "$f(\\sqrt{2}-1)=\\sqrt{2}+1$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "tpOKU",
      "text": "$f(\\sqrt{2}-1)=\\sqrt{2}$",
      "isCorrect": true,
      "image": null
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "If a function $f(x)$ assumes only irrational values which is also continuous, then $f(x)$ must be constant function.\r\n$\\Rightarrow \\quad f(x)=\\sqrt{2}$\r\n$$\r\n[\\because f(\\sqrt{2})=\\sqrt{2} \\mid\r\n$$\r\n$\\therefore \\quad f(\\sqrt{2}-1)=\\sqrt{2}$",
    "image": null
  },
